-- facts/layering.rofl — THE DECLARATIONS FOR THE ENGINE/LIBRARY SPLIT.
--
-- The owner's, like `layer(L)` and `role_decl(R)`. An executor may not add a
-- library to make a crossing legal, and may not widen the surface to make an
-- audit go quiet.

-- ============================================================== THE SURFACE
-- WHAT A LIBRARY MAY ENTER THE ENGINE THROUGH. Two files, and the second is
-- there for the storage port rather than for a domain library.
--
-- MEASURED: 26 of the 28 imports the JS model makes into `src/` already go to
-- `src/api.ts`. The surface is small because the tree already treats it as
-- small, not because a rule was imposed.
surface_decl("src/api.ts").
surface_decl("src/store.ts").
-- AND `boot.rofl`, WHICH THE FIRST RUN GOT WRONG. It reported 29 deep
-- crossings and 26 of them were a library LOADING THE SEMANTICS — which is not
-- reaching into the engine's insides, it is the engine's front door in its
-- other form. A library that could not load boot.rofl could not use the
-- language at all. The tell was that the audit named one target 26 times: an
-- audit whose rows are mostly one destination is usually wrong about that
-- destination rather than right about the sources.
surface_decl("boot.rofl").

-- ============================================================= THE LIBRARIES
-- `library_decl(Name, PathPrefix)`. One library today; the whole point of the
-- decision is that there will be more, and a second one costs four facts.
library_decl(js, "rules/js-").
library_decl(js, "facts/js-").
library_decl(js, "scanners/js").
library_decl(js, "test/js-").
library_decl(js, "test/fixtures/js-").

-- ================================================================== RULINGS
-- `side_decl(Path, Side)` overrides location. Empty on the first pass: every
-- assignment below is currently by location, and a ruling here should be a
-- decision somebody made after reading the audit.

-- ======================================================= THE READ INTERFACE
-- `kernel_reads(Rel)` — the relations a PROGRAM writes and the kernel reads, or
-- the reverse. Eighteen names, and they are the whole contract between the two.
--
-- DECLARED AND NOT DERIVED, on purpose, and it is the one list in this file
-- that cannot be computed. `RESERVED` in src/reflect.ts is what the kernel
-- OWNS and is readable off the running code; the destructor table likewise.
-- These are the opposite: relations the kernel deliberately does NOT own, so
-- there is nothing in the kernel to read them off. Parsing `policy.rofl` and
-- `safety.rofl` for their heads over-answers — it returns `analysed`, `slot`,
-- `binds_at` and twelve more that are those programs' own internals and no
-- part of any contract.
--
-- IT LIVED IN `scripts/kernel_grep.ts` UNTIL 2026-09-10, inside a check that
-- was deleted. Eighteen names in a declaration a reader can see at once beats
-- eighteen names inside a 120-entry whitelist that also held host tags, REPL
-- words, tokenizer tags — and `in_perspective`, which the kernel had removed.
edb(kernel_reads).
kernel_reads(stratum).            -- the phase a relation settles in
kernel_reads(unstratified).       -- the stock evaluator's refusal
kernel_reads(semantics).          -- the program asks for the alternating fixpoint
kernel_reads(unknown).            -- the kernel writes what that fixpoint leaves open
kernel_reads(sealed).             -- the floor-sealing declaration
kernel_reads(rule_reads).         -- policy.rofl, from here down
kernel_reads(rule_relation).
kernel_reads(cone).
kernel_reads(opaque_closed).
kernel_reads(opaque_seed).        -- and this one the host seeds
kernel_reads(unsafe_rule).        -- safety.rofl, from here down
kernel_reads(premise_var).        -- seeded by the host
kernel_reads(slot_arity).         -- seeded by the host
kernel_reads(late_rule).
kernel_reads(demand_rel).
kernel_reads(trigger_of).
kernel_reads(neg_relation).
kernel_reads(provenance_reader).
